From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Noronha Silva <gustavo@noronha.dev.br>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 13:18:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] virgl: let planar YUV formats be looked up in the
 sampler bitmask

util_format describes a planar YUV format by its planes and gives it no
channels, so util_format_get_first_non_void_channel returns -1 and
virgl_is_format_supported rejects NV12/I420/NV21/YV12 before it ever
consults the host's capabilities.

The gallium frontend reads that as "no native YUV sampling" and imports
each plane as its own pipe_resource. Every plane of a single-fd dma-buf
shares one virtio-gpu resource handle, and a sampler view carries only a
format -- so I420's two chroma planes, identical in format and in size,
are indistinguishable on the wire. That layout cannot be made to work;
the native path, one resource and one view in the composite format, can.

Ask the sampler bitmask instead, so a host that does advertise these
formats gets used.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c
index 1e0b7cba1bf..aabb7ad44e2 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c
@@ -747,6 +747,15 @@ virgl_is_format_supported( struct pipe_screen *screen,
      goto out_lookup;
    }
 
+   /* Planar YUV has no channels to inspect -- util_format describes it by its
+    * planes -- so the generic checks below would reject it out of hand and the
+    * gallium frontend would fall back to importing each plane as its own
+    * resource. Whether the host can sample the composite format is exactly
+    * what the sampler bitmask says, so go ask it. */
+   if (format_desc->layout == UTIL_FORMAT_LAYOUT_PLANAR2 ||
+       format_desc->layout == UTIL_FORMAT_LAYOUT_PLANAR3)
+      goto out_lookup;
+
    i = util_format_get_first_non_void_channel(format);
    if (i == -1)
       return false;
